My primary argument is a very secular take on what certain other
people consider a grave's headstone. The example with walks in
cementaries is intended to show secular is not the same as
desecrative.

(And, by the way, I think "Izchak Miller coded for it" is a logically
flawed argument. Dead people are not around anymore, so it is
irrelevant what they would have wanted. In real life, it is of course
important what the survivors need, to ease their grief. That can very
well be "what would the deceased have wanted". I advanced the argument
only because it is was easier to use a bad assumption in my favor than
to debate its merit.)

